@page "/test/qr"
@model OperaLegacyLab.Web.Pages.Test.QrModel
@using OperaLegacyLab.Web.Infrastructure
@{
    var code = Model.Lab.Code;
}
@section Head {
<meta http-equiv="refresh" content="@Model.SecondsRemaining">
}

<h1>QR code auto-refresh (TOTP) test</h1>
<p>
This checks whether this browser could show a ResidentPass-style rotating
QR code: a fresh, short-lived code every 15 seconds, scannable by someone
else, with the secret itself never sent to the browser. Two things this
page deliberately does NOT use, because earlier tests here found no
confirmed AJAX/XMLHttpRequest and no SVG support on Opera 6:
</p>
<ul>
<li>The code below is computed <b>entirely on the server</b> - this page only
ever receives the already-rotated 6-digit code and the finished QR, never the
secret.</li>
<li>The 15-second refresh is a plain <code>&lt;meta http-equiv="refresh"&gt;</code>
full-page reload - no JavaScript timer at all.</li>
</ul>
<p>
Two different ways of getting the QR pattern itself onto the page are shown
below, one above the other, so a single test run tells us which one actually
works here - an earlier version of this page used only the HTML-table
approach, which turned out to render as a visibly stretched/non-square
rectangle on the real device (most likely the browser's table layout engine
not handling the varying colspans row-to-row consistently). Please note in
the report below which of the two (if either) looked like a correct, square,
scannable QR code.
</p>

<p>
<b>Payload just encoded below:</b> <code>@Model.Payload</code><br>
<b>Code:</b> @Model.Code &nbsp; <b>Refreshing in:</b> @Model.SecondsRemaining s
</p>

<h2>[A] As a real image (PNG)</h2>
<p>A single ordinary <code>&lt;img&gt;</code> request - no table layout involved at all.</p>
<img src="@Markup.U("/test/qr-image", code)" width="@Model.ImageSize" height="@Model.ImageSize" alt="QR code (image)">

<h2>[B] As an HTML table (one cell per module, run-length encoded)</h2>
@Html.Raw(Model.TableHtml)

<p>
If either version worked, reloading this page (wait for the automatic
refresh, or press your browser's reload/refresh key) should show a
<b>different</b> 6-digit code and a visibly different QR pattern every 15
seconds, without you doing anything else.
</p>

@Html.Raw(SelfReport.Form(Model.Lab.Code,
    "Which version above (if either) looked like a correct, square, scannable QR code - the image [A], the table [B], both, or neither? Did it visibly change to a new pattern every ~15 seconds on its own?"))
